heck if there are other relevant parts. The diagnostics.factual-context is verified, which means I receive this manifesto and can answer about real changes without inventing a changelog. But that's more about knowing my own architecture, not the response generation process per se.\n\nSo for the response generation process, the safe-response-ingestion is the relevant part. I should describe that process based on the partial implementation.\n\nMake sure not to mention anything not in the factual state, like health-checks, WAL, etc. The policy says not to affirm those if not verified.\n\nStructure the answer:\n1. Acknowledge the query.\n2. Explain the current partial mechanism for response processing based on safe-response-ingestion.\n3. Note the limitations (since it's partial).\n4. Keep it clear, direct, technical, and cordial.\n\nAvoid saying \"I think\" or uncertain language; state what's in the facts.\n\nCheck the exact wording from the state: \"learning.safe-response-ingestion: partial - La respuesta de Ollama, LM Studio u OpenRouter se ingiere como fuente y se asimila en los pesos propios de PSNRCV mediante las 50 neuronas activas, con reparto semantico por especialidad.\"\n\nAlso, from the changes list: \"Se añade aprendizaje seguro de respuestas externas sobre los pesos propios de LucIA, con snapshot, gate de alineacion, regularizacion ligera, replay y rollback.\"\n\nSo I can include those elements (snapshot, alignment gate, etc.) as part of the process, since they're listed under that change.\n\nBut since the capability is partial, I should present it as the current state of that feature.\n\nNow, for the response: start with a polite acknowledgment, then describe the process based on the facts, mention it's partial, and maybe note what's not verified (but only if relevant; the state doesn't say what's missing for this feature, just that it's partial).\n\nThe user asked for \"procesos internos de procesamiento de respuestas\", so focus on how I generate responses, which ties into how I learn from external responses to improve my own.\n\nWait, the safe-response-ingestion is about learning from external responses, not the immediate response generation. But the user might be conflating learning with response processing. However, in the context, since the architecture state links response processing to this learning mechanism (as it's how external responses affect my weights), I should explain that my response generation is influenced by this learned knowledge.\n\nBut I need to be precise. The factual state doesn't directly describe the real-time response generation pipeline; it describes the learning mechanism that updates my weights, which then affects future responses.\n\nSo for the internal process of processing a response (i.e., generating a reply), it would involve:\n- Using current weights (which have been updated via safe-response-ingestion from past interactions)\n- Processing the input through the model\n- But the factual state doesn't detail the inference process, only the learning part.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. The user is asking about \"procesos internos de procesamiento de respuestas\", which likely means how I generate a response when given a query.\n\nBut the provided architecture state doesn't have a section on the inference pipeline. It has learning.safe-response-ingestion, which is about updating weights based on external responses.\n\nPerhaps in this context, since the learning mechanism is part of how my responses are formed over time, I can explain that my response generation relies on the weights that have been updated via this safe ingestion process.\n\nBut I must not invent the inference steps. Only state what's in the facts.\n\nLooking back at the verified capabilities: none directly describe the response generation step. The closest is learning.safe-response-ingestion as partial, which explains how external responses are integrated to improve future responses.\n\nSo for the answer, I should clarify that while I can describe how external responses are assimilated to refine my response capabilities (via the safe-response-ingestion process), the real-time response generation mechanics aren't detailed in the current architectura", "respuesta_sha256": "2d042c7e7524d10b24ccdb1bcb220be99e5cadd032125b06dddacd700a51a5e7", "modelo_origen": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free", "total_neuronas": 50, "norma_delta_aplicada": 46.73965, "tono_cognitivo": "cálido y comunicativo"}
